# tools/ales_stereo_paste_over_witness.rish — stereo_paste_over: the overwrite paste carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep, validate-both-before-either (ALES121).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES121.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-021504_fill-ales121-lotus-stereo-paste-over.md
# Stands on: lotus/paste_over.rye (ALES114 — the mono overwrite paste run once per channel), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip, two mono Clips left/right, and its balance invariant),
# lotus/stereo_replace.rye (ALES116 — the insert-mode stereo paste used to prove the insert-contrast law), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip, its max_clip bound, and the EditError
# reused whole), lotus/meter.rye (ALES13 — the peak/RMS meter). A Lotus master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length so the channels stay
# aligned in time; punching a clean take over a flubbed bar lands on both speakers at once. This rung carries ALES114's paste_over into stereo the way ALES116 carried replace and ALES120
# carried duplicate: run the proven mono paste_over on each channel over the same span. Where stereo_replace inserts a payload and can resize, stereo_paste_over writes ON TOP and keeps the length.
#
# lotus/stereo_paste_over.rye writes left_src over [at, at+left_src.len) in the left channel and right_src over the same span in the right, validating BOTH channels before either is
# mutated (a stereo payload names one span across both channels, so its two sides must be the same length — BadRange otherwise — and the mono preconditions are checked once against the
# shared length up front) so a refusal never desynchronises the channels. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO OVERWRITE LAW: each channel equals ALES114's mono paste_over on that channel
# byte for byte, the length held. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after, both holding their starting length. THE INSERT-CONTRAST LAW: the same payloads keep the length
# here and grow both channels through ALES116's stereo_replace(count 0). THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced, empty payloads the identity,
# mismatched payloads BadRange.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_paste_over_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-paste-over: Language EN — stereo_paste_over writes left_src over the span [at, at+left_src.len) in the left channel of a StereoClip and right_src over the same span in the right, so the overwrite paste ALES114 named for a mono clip now lands on a real stereo master. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length so the two channels stay aligned in time; punching a clean take over a flubbed bar lands on both speakers at once. Where stereo_replace (ALES116) inserts a payload and can resize the channels, stereo_paste_over writes on top and keeps the length — the length-preserving paste mode. It carries ALES114's proven mono paste_over into stereo — the same span on each channel — so the write lands identically in both and the stereo image stays balanced."
say "ales-stereo-paste-over: Style Radiant — the rule is stated positively: run the mono paste_over per channel over its public API, validating both channels before either is mutated (a stereo payload names one span across both channels, so its two sides must be the same length — BadRange otherwise — and the mono preconditions are checked once against the shared length up front), so a refusal never leaves one channel written and the other not. The crux stands out as four laws: the stereo overwrite law (each channel equals ALES114's mono paste_over on that channel byte for byte, every sample outside the span unchanged), the balance / length law (left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length, the stereo image staying aligned), the insert-contrast law (the same payloads keep the length here and grow both channels through ALES116's stereo_replace with count 0), and the atomicity / degenerate law (any refusal leaves both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, empty payloads the identity on both, mismatched payloads BadRange)."
say "ales-stereo-paste-over: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) whose named span is overwritten in lockstep; the payload is bounded once against max_clip before it names a u32 size, its two sides checked equal, and the write is checked without underflow against the common channel length once up front (at against len, count against the remainder), so every refusal comes by name before any write and the channels can never desynchronise; paste_over writes values only in one bounded in-place pass, no second buffer, so each channel holds its starting length and balance is preserved; it reuses ALES2's EditError whole and ALES114's paste_over per channel over its public API, inventing no new primitive; the stereo overwrite law is proven against ALES114's own paste_over run per channel, and the insert-contrast law sets it beside ALES116's stereo_replace(count 0); siloed to lotus/, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_paste_over"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_paste_over.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-paste-over: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_paste_over.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-paste-over: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-paste-over: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-paste-over' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-paste-over: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-paste-over line"
